// ─── The advisor ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/**
 * What the advisor has already said, and what it will say next.
 *
 * In the product every one of these is a Gemini call. Here they are rows,
 * which is the one part of the demo that is genuinely a substitute rather than
 * a snapshot: there is no key and no budget, so the analysis has to have been
 * written in advance.
 *
 * It is written to pass the same bar the prompt sets, because an obviously
 * synthetic paragraph would undermine the feature rather than demonstrate it:
 *
 *   · Every claim is checkable against the fixtures above. "ליברפול לקחו שלושה
 *     נצחונות רצופים" is true of this dataset — 2-1 over Arsenal, 2-1 at
 *     Napoli, 2-0 at Brighton — and nothing below cites a result, an injury or
 *     a table position that is not in the rows.
 *   · No percentages, no implied probability, no confidence score. The prompt
 *     forbids them and `InsightCard` has nowhere to put them.
 *   · The reasoning accounts for the fact that odds *are* points. That is why
 *     two of the five recommendations are against the favourite: at 1.38 the
 *     BTTS market in Bayern–PSG is probably right and barely worth having,
 *     while the same read of the match pays 1.80 on the winner.
 *   · Where the data is thin it says so — Chelsea have one match in the window
 *     and the analysis admits it rather than inventing a fourth sentence.
 *
 * `outcomeLabel` is the Hebrew club name and `odds` matches the fixture's own
 * price for that key, because `decorate()` takes both from our rows rather than
 * from the model: the two agree in the normal case and ours is right in the
 * abnormal one.
 */
type InsightPayload = {
  headline: string;
  recommendation: {
    question_type: QuestionType;
    outcome_key: string;
    outcomeLabel: string;
    odds: number;
  };
  reasons: string[];
};

